  "body": "Triage of harvest issue #1099, which collected two open review findings from merged pull requests. Both were verified against `develop` HEAD and both still hold.\n\n## Specification\n\n- Item writes made by the property type migration go through a choke-point-owned write primitive instead of raw `UPDATE items` statements in the migration job (PR #944).\n- A redundant `mkdir` call and its unused import are removed from the tenant entity test (PR #1095).\n\n## Decisions\n\n- **Two drafts, not one.** The findings share no code, no test and no review surface; bundling them would make a finding on the larger change block the trivial one.\n- **A new choke-point primitive instead of `updateItemWithClient` for #944.** `updateItemWithClient` merges patches with `||` and cannot remove a key, refuses soft-deleted rows, and fires hooks and heartbeats; using it would change the migration's behavior. The primitive lives in `chokePoint/`, so the choke-point stays the single owner of item writes, and it keeps the same SQL.\n- **The same bypass in `moduleDataMigration.ts` is not covered.** The finding did not flag it, and the triage fixes only what was flagged.\n- **#944 is not blocked on #1016 or #1077.** Both open issues edit `propertyTypeMigration.ts`, but in other regions (`enqueuePropertyTypeMigration` and a `console.error` line); the batch loop is untouched by either.\n\n## Rejected and already-fixed findings\n\nNone: both findings were verified `valid`.\n\n## Notes\n\nHarvest issue: #1099. Closing this epic retires no finding; each child issue carries its own findings and its own decision.\n\n<!-- crb-followup-epic:v1 harvest=1099 -->",
